<p>Dear Dr. Wang,</p>

<p>On behalf of the Program Committee, I am pleased to inform you that your paper <b>"Layered Timeout Fallback in Email Gateways: A Practical Approach to Graceful Degradation"</b> (Paper #114) has been <b style="color:#0a7d34">accepted</b> for presentation at ACM CCS 2026.</p>

<p>Your paper received the following review scores: <b>Accept (4.5/5)</b>, <b>Weak Accept (3.5/5)</b>, <b>Accept (4.0/5)</b>. The meta-review highlights "the practical evaluation on a real production email gateway with over 2M daily messages" as a significant strength.</p>

<h3 style="color:#0067b8;border-bottom:1px solid #0067b8;padding-bottom:4px">Camera-Ready Instructions</h3>

<p><b>Deadline:</b> October 17, 2026 (23:59 AoE)</p>

<ol>
<li><b>Revise your paper</b> based on the reviewer comments (available in the review system). No major revisions are required, but you should address the minor comments about the evaluation methodology in Section 4.2.</li>
<li><b>Format your paper</b> using the ACM Master Template (acmart). Use the <code>sigconf</code> format with the CCS concepts and keywords. Page limit: 12 pages + references.</li>
<li><b>Submit the copyright form</b> — ACM will send a separate email with a link to the copyright system. You (or your co-authors) must complete this before camera-ready submission.</li>
<li><b>Upload your camera-ready paper</b> to the submission system. You may use the following link:</li>
